---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

// Tous les chiffres viennent du Central Statistics Office, jeu EHQ03
// (Average Earnings, Hours Worked, Employment and Labour Costs), trimestre
// 2026Q2, releve le 2026-09-26 par l'API publique du CSO. Rien n'est estime
// ni arrondi a l'avantage d'une conclusion : l'equivalent annuel est
// simplement le montant hebdomadaire multiplie par 52, et la page le dit.
const TRIMESTRE = '2026Q2';
const RELEVE = '26 September 2026';
const HEBDO = 1046.88;
const HORAIRE = 31.96;
const HEURES = 32.8;
const ANNUEL = Math.round(HEBDO * 52);

const secteurs = [
  { nom: 'Information and communication', hebdo: 1730.61 },
  { nom: 'Financial and insurance activities', hebdo: 1556.66 },
  { nom: 'Professional, scientific and technical activities', hebdo: 1294.65 },
  { nom: 'Public administration and defence', hebdo: 1256.79 },
  { nom: 'Electricity, water supply and waste management', hebdo: 1222.55 },
  { nom: 'Manufacturing', hebdo: 1165.43 },
  { nom: 'Construction', hebdo: 1089.21 },
  { nom: 'Real estate activities', hebdo: 896.21 },
  { nom: 'Administrative and support service activities', hebdo: 838.79 },
  { nom: 'Wholesale and retail trade', hebdo: 775.71 },
  { nom: 'Arts, entertainment and recreation', hebdo: 684.00 },
  { nom: 'Other service activities', hebdo: 666.31 },
  { nom: 'Accommodation and food service activities', hebdo: 506.78 },
];
// Meme jeu CSO, deuxieme trimestre de chaque annee : comparer le meme
// trimestre evite le biais saisonnier des primes.
const serie = [
  { an: 2019, hebdo: 771.39 }, { an: 2020, hebdo: 817.34 },
  { an: 2021, hebdo: 851.14 }, { an: 2022, hebdo: 873.32 },
  { an: 2023, hebdo: 912.12 }, { an: 2024, hebdo: 964.54 },
  { an: 2025, hebdo: 1007.58 }, { an: 2026, hebdo: 1046.88 },
];
const euro = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-IE', { minimumFractionDigits: 2, maximumFractionDigits: 2 });
const annuel = (n: number) => Math.round(n * 52).toLocaleString('en-IE');
---

<Layout
  title="Average Salary in Ireland 2026: What the CSO Data Shows"
  description="Average weekly earnings reached €1,046.88 in the second quarter of 2026. What that means annually, how it splits by sector, and why the average misleads."
>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-6">
    <ol class="flex items-center gap-1">
      <li><a href="/" class="hover:text-primary">Home</a></li>
      <li class="before:content-['/'] before:mx-1">Average Salary Ireland</li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>Average Salary in Ireland 2026: What the CSO Data Shows</h1>

    <p>
      Average weekly earnings across all sectors in Ireland stood at <strong>&euro;{euro(HEBDO)}</strong>
      in {TRIMESTRE}, according to the Central Statistics Office. Multiplied across fifty-two weeks,
      that is <strong>&euro;{ANNUEL.toLocaleString('en-IE')} a year</strong> before tax. Average hourly
      earnings were &euro;{euro(HORAIRE)}, over an average paid week of {HEURES} hours. Those three
      numbers come from the same official release and describe the same workforce, which is why they
      are worth reading together rather than separately: the hourly figure looks high precisely
      because the average paid week is well under forty hours, and any comparison with a full-time
      salary elsewhere has to account for that. The headline figure is also an average across every
      employee in the economy, from part-time hospitality staff to full-time software engineers. It
      is not what a typical worker earns, it is not a median, and it is not a salary you should expect
      to be offered. What it is good for is comparison over time and between sectors, and that is how
      this page uses it.
    </p>

    <h2>The headline numbers, and what each one measures</h2>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Measure</th><th>{TRIMESTRE}</th><th>Annual equivalent</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Average weekly earnings</td><td>&euro;{euro(HEBDO)}</td><td>&euro;{ANNUEL.toLocaleString('en-IE')}</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Average hourly earnings</td><td>&euro;{euro(HORAIRE)}</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Average weekly paid hours</td><td>{HEURES} hours</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      The annual equivalent is the weekly figure multiplied by fifty-two. It is arithmetic, not a
      measurement: the CSO publishes earnings weekly and hourly, not annually. Treating the result as
      a salary assumes fifty-two paid weeks, which holds for most salaried employees in Ireland since
      annual leave is paid, but not for seasonal or irregular work.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gap between the hourly and weekly figures is the part most readers miss. At
      &euro;{euro(HORAIRE)} an hour, a standard thirty-nine hour week would produce roughly
      &euro;{euro(HORAIRE * 39)}. The published weekly average is lower because the average paid week
      is {HEURES} hours, pulled down by part-time employment, which is concentrated in particular
      sectors rather than spread evenly.
    </p>

    <h2>Average weekly earnings by sector</h2>

    <p>
      The spread between sectors is far wider than any change over time, which is why a single
      national average tells you so little about your own position.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Sector</th><th>Weekly</th><th>Annual equivalent</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {secteurs.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{s.nom}</td>
            <td>&euro;{euro(s.hebdo)}</td>
            <td>&euro;{annuel(s.hebdo)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Information and communication sits at &euro;{euro(1730.61)} a week, accommodation and food
      service at &euro;{euro(506.78)}. That is a ratio of more than three to one between the highest
      and lowest sectors published. Someone working in hospitality and someone working in software
      are both included in the &euro;{euro(HEBDO)} national average, and neither would recognise it as
      describing their pay.
    </p>
    <p>
      Part of the gap is hours rather than rates. Accommodation and food service averages twenty-eight
      paid hours a week against the national {HEURES}, so the weekly figure understates the hourly
      rate in that sector. Comparing sectors on weekly earnings alone therefore overstates the
      difference in pay per hour, though a substantial difference remains after that adjustment.
    </p>

    <h2>How the average has moved since 2019</h2>

    <p>
      Comparing the same quarter each year removes the seasonal effect of bonuses, which land
      unevenly across the year and distort quarter-to-quarter comparisons.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Quarter</th><th>Weekly</th><th>Annual equivalent</th><th>Change on previous year</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {serie.map((r, i) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{r.an}Q2</td>
            <td>&euro;{euro(r.hebdo)}</td>
            <td>&euro;{annuel(r.hebdo)}</td>
            <td>{i === 0 ? '—' : `+${(100 * (r.hebdo - serie[i - 1].hebdo) / serie[i - 1].hebdo).toFixed(1)} %`}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Average weekly earnings rose from &euro;{euro(771.39)} in 2019Q2 to &euro;{euro(HEBDO)} in
      {TRIMESTRE}, an increase of {(100 * (HEBDO - 771.39) / 771.39).toFixed(1)} per cent across seven
      years. That is the nominal change: it says nothing about what those euros buy. Comparing it with
      consumer price inflation over the same period is the only way to tell whether pay has kept pace,
      and that comparison is outside what this dataset supports.
    </p>
    <p>
      The two largest annual rises in the series fall in 2020 and 2024. The 2020 figure deserves
      particular caution: employment composition changed sharply that year, and when lower-paid roles
      leave the measured workforce the average rises without anyone receiving a pay increase. An
      average is sensitive to who is counted, not only to what they are paid.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the average is not the typical salary</h2>

    <p>
      Three things separate the published average from what a given person earns, and all three push
      in the same direction.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It is a mean, not a median.</strong> A small number of very high earners lifts a mean
      well above the midpoint of the distribution. More people earn below the average than above it.
      The CSO release used here publishes means; anyone quoting it as what most people earn has
      misread it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It mixes full-time and part-time.</strong> The {HEURES} hour average paid week is the
      clearest evidence of this. A figure that blends a thirty-nine hour contract with a fifteen hour
      one describes neither.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>It is national, not local.</strong> Sector composition differs across the country, and
      the sectors that pay most are not evenly distributed. A national average carries that
      composition with it.
    </p>

    <h2>From gross average to what actually arrives</h2>

    <p>
      A gross figure is not take-home pay. On &euro;{ANNUEL.toLocaleString('en-IE')} a year, an
      employee in Ireland pays income tax at the standard and higher rates depending on where the
      standard rate cut-off point falls, plus USC and PRSI, and receives tax credits that reduce the
      bill directly rather than reducing taxable income.
    </p>
    <p>
      The difference between the gross headline and the net amount is large enough that comparing job
      offers on gross alone is unreliable, particularly across the cut-off point where each additional
      euro is taxed at the higher rate. Our <a href="/">take-home pay calculator</a> applies the
      current bands, credits, USC rates and PRSI to a gross figure and shows what remains, and our
      <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> sets out exactly which rates it uses and where they
      come from.
    </p>

    <h2>Where these figures come from</h2>

    <p>
      Every number on this page comes from the Central Statistics Office release
      <em>Average Earnings, Hours Worked, Employment and Labour Costs</em>, dataset EHQ03, for
      {TRIMESTRE}, retrieved from the CSO public data API on {RELEVE}. Sector figures are for all
      employees within each NACE Rev. 2 sector.
    </p>
    <p>
      Annual equivalents are our own arithmetic, weekly earnings multiplied by fifty-two, and are
      labelled as such throughout. The CSO does not publish an annual average salary figure, and any
      site presenting one as official has calculated it the same way without saying so.
    </p>
    <p>
      The CSO revises quarterly earnings data as later returns arrive, so figures for recent quarters
      can change. This page states the quarter and the retrieval date precisely so that a reader can
      check it against the current release rather than trusting it indefinitely.
    </p>

    <h2>Common questions</h2>

    <h3>Is &euro;{ANNUEL.toLocaleString('en-IE')} a good salary in Ireland?</h3>
    <p>
      It is the average, which means a majority of employees earn less. Whether it is good depends
      entirely on sector, hours and location, and the sector table above shows a spread of more than
      three to one. A better question is what the average is in the sector you work in, which is why
      that breakdown is on this page.
    </p>

    <h3>Why is the average hourly rate so high compared with the weekly figure?</h3>
    <p>
      Because the average paid week is {HEURES} hours, not thirty-nine. Part-time employment pulls the
      weekly average down without affecting the hourly rate. If you work full-time hours, the hourly
      figure is closer to your reality than the weekly one.
    </p>

    <h3>Does this include bonuses and overtime?</h3>
    <p>
      Yes. The CSO series used here covers total earnings, which includes irregular earnings such as
      bonuses and overtime. The CSO also publishes a series excluding irregular earnings, which runs
      lower and is the better measure for comparing base pay between sectors.
    </p>

    <h3>How often is this updated?</h3>
    <p>
      The CSO publishes this series quarterly. This page states the quarter it reflects and the date
      the figures were retrieved, and both appear in the section above so you can tell at a glance
      whether a newer release exists.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
